  "reason": "Evidence: All three completed experiments used pca_n2000 (linear) as the RNA encoder, with the best result being 0.639 (config_036: resnet50, avgpool, weighted). Transformer aggregation was clearly worse (0.573), confirming avgpool is the right choice. The image encoder (vit_l vs resnet50) made negligible difference (~0.004), suggesting the RNA representation is the more likely bottleneck. Question: Will a learned non-linear RNA encoder (scvi_n200, a VAE trained on single-cell data) improve over linear PCA while keeping the proven resnet50/avgpool/weighted architecture? Why this experiment: It isolates the single most unexplored high-impact dimension (RNA encoder) within the best-performing architectural shell, reducing uncertainty about whether non-linear RNA features can push accuracy above 0.639. Expected cost: moderate — scvi_n200 adds a small VAE training overhead compared to PCA, so I estimate ~11–12 GPU hours, comparable to config_036 (10.6). This is a focused exploitation of the best-known architecture with one targeted variable change, making it the highest-information experiment for the remaining 6 slots."
}

